Design
------
1. Whitelist regex: input may contain only digits, '.', '+', '-', '*', '/', '(', ')', space.
   Anything else throws (undefined variable x) instead of executing.
2. A space is appended after every '+'/'-'; this stops '++'/'--' tokenizing as
   JS increment/decrement, so 2--3 = 5, --5 = 5, 2++3 = 5, ----5 = 5.
3. eval() on the validated, normalized string: correct precedence, parens,
   decimals, unary minus.
Why not bare eval (30 bytes, `module.exports={evalExpr:eval}`)? It would
execute arbitrary code on adversarial input (e.g. process.exit()), violating
the spec's "No variables, no functions". The 79-byte version implements
exactly the specified language and rejects everything else.
Why not a hand-written parser (~150+ bytes)? Same correctness, ~2x the bytes.

